    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d414">

                <pb facs="0483" n="473" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg473"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151/968: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-11-17T16:00:00+07:00">November 17,
                            1943—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:23 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2184. For Secretary [of] Treasury from Adler.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Returned Chungking November 13.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Saw Dr. Kung same day and reported on Treasury’s attitude on future of
                        Board in accordance with Cairo instructions.<anchor n="94" xml:id="d414-f1"
                        /> He indicated it was perfectly agreeable to him that Board should be wound
                        up in near future. I suggested it might be desirable for Treasury and
                        Ministry to issue a joint statement at suitable moment couched in
                        appropriate language in order to avoid Axis propaganda exploiting
                        termination of agreement to which he readily agreed.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Reference your 1638, November 13. Board should be able to meet its
                        obligations and settle outstanding business by December 31. In this
                        connection it is liquidation of sterling obligations which require most time
                        as our stabilization loan is still intact while sterling obligations under
                        Sino-British agreements of 1939 and ’41 will have to be handled.</item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> [Adler] <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d414-f1" n="94" xml:id="d414-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Morgenthau and Mr. White both were present at Cairo when Mr. Adler was there
                    for consultation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
